# pigate.conf.example
#
# This is a fully-commented sample of PiGate's bootstrap runtime config file,
# normally installed at /var/lib/pigate/pigate.conf and read via the -config
# CLI flag (default path: /var/lib/pigate/pigate.conf — see cmd/pigate/main.go
# and backend/internal/config/config.go). It is NOT the SQLite-backed
# subsystem configuration (interfaces, firewall, DHCP, etc.) — only the small
# set of process bootstrap parameters historically set via CLI flags in the
# systemd unit's ExecStart line.
#
# Syntax: one "key=value" pair per line. Lines starting with '#' (after
# trimming leading whitespace) are comments; blank lines are ignored. No
# quoting is supported — a value is taken verbatim up to end of line, and may
# itself contain '=' (e.g. a path with a query-string-like suffix), because
# the line is split on the FIRST '=' only.
#
# Precedence (low to high): built-in code default < this config file < a CLI
# flag explicitly passed on the command line. A flag you actually pass on the
# command line always wins over the same key in this file.
#
# Real-world gotcha: install.sh's systemd ExecStart line keeps
# "-mock=false -db=/var/lib/pigate/pigate.db -https-port=443" as
# belt-and-suspenders CLI flags. Because an explicitly-passed flag always
# wins, editing "mock", "db", or "https-port" in this file has NO EFFECT
# while those three flags remain in the systemd unit — you must edit the
# unit (or drop those three flags from it) if you want this file to control
# them instead.

# Port to run the API server on.
port=2479

# Path to SQLite database file.
db=pigate.db
# install.sh writes db=/var/lib/pigate/pigate.db in production (see the
# ExecStart gotcha above).

# Use mocked kernel operations (default true on PC).
mock=true
# install.sh writes mock=false in production (see the ExecStart gotcha
# above).

# Mock operations but initialize/pull from real kernel data at startup.
mock-from-real=false

# Disable edit operations (Read-only mode).
disable-edit=false

# Allow editing and deleting system predefined static routes.
allow-edit-system-routes=false

# Enable direct kernel management of system/kernel-only routes without
# database.
enable-edit-system-route=false

# Prioritize kernel route information over database if duplicate.
prioritize-kernel-routes=false

# Enable Docker compatibility (bypass docker0 and br-* interfaces). Off by
# default; opt in only on a gateway that also runs Docker/bridge networks.
docker-compat=false

# HTTPS port (0 = HTTP only; the systemd unit passes 443 to make HTTPS the
# primary channel).
https-port=0
# install.sh writes https-port=443 in production (see the ExecStart gotcha
# above).

# Directory for the self-signed TLS cert/key (default: <dir of -db>/tls).
tls-dir=

# Echo CORS headers for frontend dev-server origins (localhost:5173/3000).
# Off by default; only needed when running `yarn dev` against this backend.
allow-dev-cors=false

# --- FILE-ONLY keys (no CLI flag) -------------------------------------------
# The two keys below are intentionally NOT settable via any CLI flag — they
# are RAM-tuning knobs for an appliance, not day-to-day CLI switches (see
# docs/ref/todo/dns-stats-tracking-limits-config-plan.md). They only take
# effect through this file.
#
# Both bound the DNS Query Statistics ring (backend/internal/service/
# dns_query_stats.go): a 5-minute-granularity bucket, 288 buckets deep (24h
# total window). The cap applies PER BUCKET, independently for each of the
# two dimensions below.
#
# Raising either value raises the worst-case RAM ceiling roughly linearly
# (value x 288 tracked entries in the absolute worst case — i.e. only under a
# sustained unique-domain/unique-client flood; normal home/office traffic
# sits orders of magnitude below the cap).
#
# The cap only affects the per-domain/per-client BREAKDOWN (Top Domains, Top
# Clients, and the drilldown views) — the total query count
# (TotalQueries in /api/statistics/dns) is NEVER affected by either cap, and
# the UI flags a capped window as "Truncated" so the breakdown is known to be
# incomplete without hiding that queries still happened.
#
# Validation: a non-numeric value here (e.g. "abc") is a fatal startup error,
# same as any other integer key. An out-of-range value (<=0, or above the
# internal RAM-guard sanity ceiling) is NOT fatal — it is clamped back to the
# default with a warning logged at startup, so a typo'd value can never turn
# into a boot loop that takes the gateway's network off the air.

# Maximum number of distinct (domain, client) pairs tracked per 5-minute
# bucket in the DNS Query Statistics ring.
dns-stats-max-pairs=2400

# Maximum number of distinct clients tracked per 5-minute bucket in the DNS
# Query Statistics ring (enforced independently of dns-stats-max-pairs).
dns-stats-max-clients=200

# The three keys below are the same kind of file-only RAM-tuning knob, this
# time bounding the Statistics -> Traffic page's bucket ring (backend/
# internal/service/traffic_stats.go — docs/ref/todo/
# statistics-traffic-page-plan.md §1.6). Same 5-minute/288-bucket (24h) ring,
# same per-bucket cap semantics, same clamp+warn (not fatal) out-of-range
# validation as dns-stats-max-pairs/dns-stats-max-clients above.
#
# Worst-case RAM note: a conversation entry costs ~110 bytes (≈45-char key +
# 16 B dirBytes + map overhead), and the ring holds 288 five-minute buckets,
# so traffic-stats-max-conversations=600 (the default) => 600 x 288 x 110B ≈
# 19 MB worst case (only under a sustained port-scan/P2P flood saturating
# every bucket for a full day — normal home/office traffic sits far below
# this). traffic-stats-max-dests follows the same math (~10 MB at its
# default of 500) and traffic-stats-max-hosts likewise (~12 MB at its default
# of 500).

# Maximum number of distinct source-host IPs tracked per 5-minute bucket in
# the Traffic bucket ring (Top Source Hosts / drill-down).
traffic-stats-max-hosts=500

# Maximum number of distinct destination IPs tracked per 5-minute bucket in
# the Traffic bucket ring (Top Destinations / drill-down).
traffic-stats-max-dests=500

# Maximum number of distinct (src, dst, proto, dstPort) conversations tracked
# per 5-minute bucket in the Traffic bucket ring (Top Conversations /
# drill-down).
traffic-stats-max-conversations=600
